   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div style=\"font-size:16px;margin:12px 0px 16px;border-bottom:0px rgb(10,10,10)\"><font face=\"Sans Serif\">Hi,</font></div><div style=\"font-size:16px;margin:12px 0px 16px;border-bottom:0px rgb(10,10,10)\"><font face=\"Sans Serif\">I am currently setting up a joint model with INLABRU, where one of the likelihoods is left-censored due to a limit of detection. </font><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Sans Serif&quot;\">I already looked into a </span><span dir=\"ltr\" style=\"font-family:&quot;Sans Serif&quot;;font-size:14px;margin:0px;border-bottom:1px solid rgb(240,242,245)\">lognormal.surv</span><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Sans Serif&quot;\"> and </span><span dir=\"ltr\" style=\"font-family:&quot;Sans Serif&quot;;font-size:14px;margin:0px;border-bottom:1px solid rgb(240,242,245)\">loglogisticsurv</span><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Sans Serif&quot;\">, but I need more distributional flexibility to capture the shape of my data.</span></div><div style=\"font-size:16px;margin:12px 0px 16px;border-bottom:0px rgb(10,10,10)\"><font face=\"Sans Serif\">Is it possible to use a </font><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Sans Serif&quot;\">3-parameter weibull or a mixture of distributions (lognormalsurv &amp; lognormal) within INLABRU? Or other flexible options to consider?</span></div><div style=\"font-size:16px;margin:12px 0px 16px;border-bottom:0px rgb(10,10,10)\"><font face=\"Sans Serif\">Kind regards,</font></div><div style=\"font-size:16px;margin:12px 0px 16px;border-bottom:0px rgb(10,10,10)\"><font face=\"Sans Serif\">Jonas Meijerink</font></div><div style=\"font-family:&quot;Google Sans&quot;,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:16px;margin:12px 0px 16px;border-bottom:0px rgb(10,10,10)\"></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
